Here is a sample Delphi code to find silence places in a standard WAV file using the RMS method:
```
var
  FFileStream: TFileStream;
  FBuffer: TBytes;
  SILenceThreshold: Single = 0.1; // Silence threshold in RMS

begin
  // Open the WAV file
  FFileStream := TFileStream.Create(FileName, fmOpenRead);
  try
    // Read the WAV file data into a buffer
    FBuffer := TBytes.Create;
    FFileStream.ReadBuffer(FBuffer, 0, FBuffer.Size);
    // Calculate the average RMS level of the buffer
    SetLength(FBuffer, 0);
    for I := 0 to High(FBuffer) do
      for J := I to High(FBuffer) do
        if FBuffer[J] <> 0 then
          FBuffer[J] := FBuffer[J] - (FBuffer[J] ^ (FBuffer[J] + FBuffer[J + 1]));
    RMS := (sum(FBuffer.^2) / Length(FBuffer));
    // Find the silence places
    for I := 0 to High(FBuffer) do
      if FBuffer[I] < SILenceThreshold * RMS then
        // Silence place found, start a new buffer
        if I > 0 then
          for J := I - 1 to High(FBuffer) do
            FBuffer[J] := 0;
        FBuffer[0] := FBuffer[0] + FBuffer[1];
        Inc(FBuffer, 1);
  finally
    FFileStream.Free;
  end;
end;
```
This code reads the entire WAV file into memory, then calculates the average RMS level of the data. It then iterates through the buffer, marking the positions where the RMS level drops below the threshold as silence places. The silence places are stored in a new buffer that starts at the beginning of the silence and ends at the end of the silence.

Note that this code assumes that the silence places in the WAV file are separated by at least one sample (1/2 second) in order to avoid false positives. You

.then() and .finally() are two methods on Promise objects in JavaScript. They do two different things:

.then()

.then() is used to handle the resolved value or rejected reason of a promise. It takes two arguments, a resolve handler and a reject handler:

```js
promise.then(
  value => {
    // resolve handler, handles resolved promise value
  },
  reason => {
    // reject handler, handles promise rejection reason
  }
)
```

The resolve handler will be called if the promise resolves, and the reject handler will be called if the promise rejects.

.finally()

.finally() is used to execute code whether the promise resolves or rejects. It takes a single handler function that will always be executed:

```js
promise.finally(() => {
  // will always be executed
})
```

The key difference is that .then() handles the promise resolution and rejection, while .finally() will always execute regardless of the promise outcome.

Some examples:

```js
fetch('/user/1')
  .then(res => res.json())
  .then(user => {
    // user object
  })
  .catch(err => {
    // handle error
  })
  .finally(() => {
    // always executed
  })

doSomething()
  .then(result => {
    // success
  })
  .finally(() => {
    // always executed
  })
```

In the first example, .finally() will run after the promise resolves or rejects. 
In the second example, .finally() will run after the promise resolves.

So in summary:

.then() - Handles promise resolution and rejection 
.finally() - Always executed regardless of promise outcome

Use .then() when you want to handle the resolved value or rejected reason of a promise.
Use .finally() when you want to execute some code whether a promise resolves or rejects.

NAME_2, é possível ter construtores assíncronos em Node.js! Isso pode ser feito de duas maneiras:

1. Usando uma função assíncrona como construtor:

```js
async function NAME_1() {
  // construtor assíncrono
}
```

Nesse caso, você pode chamar `await` dentro do construtor e retornar uma promise. Por exemplo:

```js
async function NAME_1() {
  const result = await otherFunction();
  // ...
}
```

2. Usando uma factory function que retorna uma promise:

```js
function NAME_1() {
  return new Promise(resolve => {
    // fazer alguma coisa assíncrona 
    resolve();
  });
}
```

Nesse caso, você "constrói" a instância chamando `new NAME_1()`. Por exemplo:

```js
const NAME_1 = new NAME_1();
// Aguarde a promise ser resolvida...
```

Em ambos os casos, você pode usar o objeto instanciado normalmente depois que a promise for resolvida.

Por exemplo:

```js
async function NAME_1() {
  await doSomething();
  this.bar = 1;
}

const f = new NAME_1();
f.bar // 1
```

O construtor assíncrono irá esperar as operações assíncronas terminarem antes de considerar o objeto "construído".

Resumindo, NAME_2 é possível ter construtores assíncronos em Node.js usando:

- Funções assíncronas como construtores
- Factory functions que retornam promises

Isso permite realizar operações assíncronas durante a construção de objetos em Node.js!

