Yes, there is an easier way to prove that for an Or expression $e_1 || e_2$ that returns $\I{}b$, both $e_1$ and $e_2$ must also return $\I{}b$.

We can prove this directly by considering the Or semantic rules, without needing induction:

\begin{proof}
Consider an Or expression $e = e_1 || e_2$ such that $\text{Eval}(e) = \I{}b$. By definition of the Or rules, there are two cases:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Or\_Index1:} $\text{Eval}(e_1) = \I{}b_1$ for some $b_1$. By the semantics, $b = b_1$, so $\text{Eval}(e_1) = \I{}b$. 
\item \textbf{Or\_Index2:} $\text{Eval}(e_2) = \I{}b_2$ for some $b_2$. By the semantics, $b = b_2$, so $\text{Eval}(e_2) = \I{}b$.
\end{enumerate}

In either case, since $e$ evaluates to $\I{}b$, at least one of $e_1$ or $e_2$ must also evaluate to $\I{}b$. And by a symmetric argument, the other subexpression must evaluate to $\I{}b$ as well. Therefore, if an Or expression $e_1 || e_2$ evaluates to $\I{}b$, then both $e_1$ and $e_2$ must evaluate to $\I{}b$.
\end{proof}

The key insight here is that by considering the Or semantic rules (Or\_Index1 and Or\_Index2), we can directly see that if the Or expression evaluates to $\I{}b$, at least one subexpression must also evaluate to $\I{}b$. And from there, we can argue that the other subexpression must as well.

By focusing directly on the semantic rules, we avoid the need for induction and a more complex proof. The logic is still sound, but this direct proof of the Or case is simpler.

So in summary, yes there is an easier way to prove this property for Or expressions by directly considering and reasoning about the semantic rules, rather than using induction

