\indent \textbf{Theorem:} If $e = e_1 \&\& e_2$ and $\text{Eval}(e) = \I{}b$, then $\text{Eval}(e_1) = \text{Eval}(e_2) = \I{}b$.

\indent \textbf{Proof:} By definition, $\text{Eval}(e) = \I{}b$ means $e$ evaluates to some indexed boolean value $b$. There are two rules for evaluating And expressions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{And\_Index:} If $\text{Eval}(e_1) = \I{}b_1$ and $\text{Eval}(e_2) = \I{}b_2$ for some $b_1, b_2$, then $\text{Eval}(e_1 \&\& e_2) = \I{}(b_1 \land b_2)$. By this rule, since $\text{Eval}(e) = \I{}b$, it must be the case that $b = (b_1 \land b_2)$ for some $b_1, b_2$. Therefore, $\text{Eval}(e_1) = \I{}b_1$ and $\text{Eval}(e_2) = \I{}b_2$.

\item \textbf{And\_NoIndex:} If $\text{Eval}(e_1) = b_1$ and $\text{Eval}(e_2) = b_2$ for some boolean $b_1, b_2$, then $\text{Eval}(e_1 \&\& e_2) = (b_1 \land b_2)$. This rule does not apply here since $\text{Eval}(e) = \I{}b$, not a non-indexed boolean. 
\end{enumerate}

Therefore, by considering the And semantic rules, the only possibility is that And\_Index applies and $\text{Eval}(e_1) = \I{}b_1$ and $\text{Eval}(e_2) = \I{}b_2$ for some $b_1, b_2$ such that $b = (b_1 \land b_2)$. This proves the theorem.

