Good catch! The function as written does risk a buffer overflow.

The potential for buffer overflow comes from this line:

`memmove(p + to_len, p + from_len, strlen(p + from_len) + 1);`

This moves data after the match to make space for the new substring. However, if the new substring is longer than the original, it could overwrite data beyond the end of the buffer.

A safer approach would be to:

1. Calculate the required new length after replacement 
2. Allocate a new buffer with that required length
3. Copy the string up to the match into the new buffer  
4. Copy the replacement substring into the new buffer
5. Continue the process until the end of the string
6. Free the original buffer and point the string pointer to the new buffer

For example:

```c
size_t new_len = strlen(str) - strlen(from) + strlen(to) + 1;
char *new_str = malloc(new_len);

char *p = str;
char *np = new_str;

while ((p = strstr(p, from))) {
   ...
   strcpy(np, str);   // Copy text before match
   np += strlen(np);
   strcpy(np, to);    // Copy replacement text
   np += strlen(to);  
   p += strlen(from); 
}

free(str);
str = new_str;
```

This allocates an exact-size buffer, copies the string in chunks, and then frees the original buffer - avoiding any buffer overflow issues.

